    {
      "emittedAtMs": 1789199579887,
      "method": "item/started",
      "params": {
        "item": {
          "clientId": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Let \\\\( x \\\\) and \\\\( y \\\\) be positive integers. If for each positive integer \\\\( n \\\\) we have that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(n y)^{2}+1 \\\\mid x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1\\n\\\\]\\n\\nprove that \\\\( x=1 \\\\).\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"bmo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidates\": [{\"candidate_id\": \"choice-1\", \"text\": \"By Dirichlet’s theorem, choose a prime \\\\(q\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod 4\\\\) such that \\\\(q>\\\\max\\\\{y,x+1\\\\}\\\\). Since \\\\(-1\\\\) is a quadratic residue modulo \\\\(q\\\\), choose \\\\(s\\\\) with \\\\(s^2\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod q\\\\). As \\\\(q\\\\nmid y\\\\), choose \\\\(a\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod q\\\\) satisfying \\\\(ay\\\\equiv s\\\\pmod q\\\\).\\n\\nFor every positive integer \\\\(n\\\\equiv a\\\\pmod q\\\\), we then have\\n\\\\[\\nq\\\\mid (ny)^2+1,\\n\\\\]\\nso the hypothesis gives\\n\\\\[\\nx^{\\\\varphi(n)}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod q.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause \\\\(q>x\\\\), let \\\\(d=\\\\operatorname{ord}_q(x)\\\\). Thus \\\\(d\\\\mid\\\\varphi(n)\\\\) for every such \\\\(n\\\\), and \\\\(d\\\\mid q-1\\\\).\\n\\nSuppose \\\\(d>2\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\gcd(d,q)=1\\\\), the Chinese remainder theorem gives a reduced residue class \\\\(c\\\\pmod{qd}\\\\) satisfying\\n\\\\[\\nc\\\\equiv a\\\\pmod q,\\\\qquad c\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod d.\\n\\\\]\\nBy Dirichlet’s theorem, this class contains a prime \\\\(r\\\\). Taking \\\\(n=r\\\\), the preceding congruence implies\\n\\\\[\\nd\\\\mid\\\\varphi(r)=r-1.\\n\\\\]\\nBut \\\\(r\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod d\\\\), so \\\\(r-1\\\\equiv-2\\\\pmod d\\\\), forcing \\\\(d\\\\mid2\\\\), a contradiction. Hence \\\\(d\\\\le2\\\\), and therefore\\n\\\\[\\nx^2\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod q.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(q\\\\mid(x-1)(x+1)\\\\). Since \\\\(1\\\\le x<q-1\\\\), this is possible only when \\\\(x-1=0\\\\). Consequently, \\\\(x=1\\\\).\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-2\", \"text\": \"Assume for contradiction that x>1. Taking n=2, since φ(2)=1, gives\\n\\n4y²+1 | x-1.\\n\\nChoose a prime q dividing 4y²+1. Then (2y)² ≡ -1 (mod q), so q≡1 (mod 4), hence q≥5, and q∤y. Also q|x-1. Put c=v_q(x-1), which is finite.\\n\\nWe prove that for every K≥1 there exists a positive integer n such that\\n\\nq^K | n²y²+1  and  q∤φ(n).\\n\\nLet f(T)=y²T²+1. We have f(2)≡0 (mod q), while f'(2)=4y² is nonzero modulo q. Thus Hensel lifting gives a residue r_K modulo q^K such that\\n\\nr_K≡2 (mod q),   f(r_K)≡0 (mod q^K).\\n\\nSet g=(q-1)². Since v_q(g-1)=1, LTE gives\\n\\nv_q(g^{q^j}-1)=j+1.\\n\\nConsequently g has order q^{K-1} modulo q^K and generates the group of residues congruent to 1 modulo q. Because r_K(q+2)^{-1}≡1 (mod q), there is some t≥0 such that\\n\\n(q+2)g^t≡r_K (mod q^K).\\n\\nDefine n=(q+2)(q-1)^{2t}. Then n≡r_K (mod q^K), so q^K|n²y²+1.\\n\\nEvery prime divisor ℓ of n divides either q-1 or q+2. Hence ℓ≠q and ℓ≢1 (mod q): indeed, the only integer in [2,q+2] congruent to 1 modulo q is q+1, which is even and greater than 2, so is not prime. From the formula for φ(n), it follows that q∤φ(n).\\n\\nNow choose K>c and take the corresponding n. The hypothesis yields\\n\\nq^K | n²y²+1 | x^{φ(n)}-1.\\n\\nOn the other hand, since q is odd, q|x-1, and q∤φ(n), LTE gives\\n\\nv_q(x^{φ(n)}-1)=v_q(x-1)+v_q(φ(n))=c<K,\\n\\na contradiction. Therefore x>1 is impossible. Since x is a positive integer, x=1.\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-3\", \"text\": \"Assume the stated divisibility holds for every positive integer n.\\n\\nFix any prime q≡1 (mod 4) with q∤y. Since −1 is a quadratic residue modulo q, choose a∈{1,…,q−1} such that (ay)^2≡−1 (mod q). Taking n=a shows q∣x^{φ(a)}−1, so q∤x. Let r=ord_q(x); then r∣q−1.\\n\\nWe claim r≤2. Otherwise r>2. Since gcd(q,r)=1, the Chinese remainder theorem gives a reduced residue class b modulo qr satisfying\\nb≡a (mod q),  b≡−1 (mod r).\\nBy Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions, choose a prime p≡b (mod qr). Then q∣(py)^2+1, so the hypothesis with n=p gives\\nq∣x^{φ(p)}−1=x^{p−1}−1.\\nHence r∣p−1. But p≡−1 (mod r), so r∣2, contradicting r>2. Thus r≤2, and consequently q∣x^2−1.\\n\\nThere are infinitely many primes q≡1 (mod 4), and only finitely many divide y. Therefore infinitely many primes divide x^2−1. This is possible only if x^2−1=0. Since x is positive, x=1.\"}]}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
